/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · Wild Nature: survivors, shapeshifters and hidden worlds */
WB.L("tardigrades", "nature", "beginner", "Tardigrades: the toughest animals on Earth",
  "Smaller than a full stop, the eight-legged 'water bear' can survive being dried out for years, frozen solid and even exposed to open space.",
  [
    ["Meet the water bear", "Tardigrades are microscopic eight-legged animals that live almost everywhere, from backyard moss to Antarctica.",
      "Scrape a clump of moss off a garden wall, soak it in water and look through a microscope, and there is a good chance you will see a tiny, plump creature lumbering along on eight stubby, clawed legs. This is a tardigrade. Most are well under a millimetre long, and the largest only just reach about a millimetre and a half. In 1773 a German pastor described one as a 'little water bear', and the name stuck. The scientific name, Tardigrada, means 'slow steppers'. More than 1,300 species have been described. They live in moss, lichen, soil, leaf litter, fresh water and ocean sediments, on mountaintops and in the deep sea, and in the polar regions. Most feed by piercing plant cells, algae or other tiny animals with sharp mouthparts."],
    ["The tun: life on pause", "When their surroundings dry out, tardigrades curl into a dried 'tun' and their metabolism almost stops.",
      "A tardigrade's watery home can vanish in a single hot afternoon, so many species have evolved a remarkable escape. As the moss dries, the animal pulls in its head and legs, loses almost all of its body water and shrivels into a little barrel called a tun. Its metabolism slows to the point where it can barely be detected. This state is called cryptobiosis, meaning 'hidden life'. In a tun, a tardigrade can wait out drought for years. When water returns, it rehydrates and, within minutes to hours, starts walking again as if nothing happened. In one well-known case, Japanese researchers revived tardigrades from a sample of Antarctic moss that had been kept frozen for more than 30 years, and one even went on to lay eggs."],
    ["Surviving the extremes", "In the tun state tardigrades can survive cold, heat, pressure and radiation that would kill almost anything else.",
      "Laboratory experiments have put dried tardigrades through astonishing ordeals. Some have survived being chilled to within a degree or so of absolute zero, the coldest temperature possible. Others have survived brief heating above the boiling point of water. They have endured pressures several times greater than at the bottom of the deepest ocean trench, and doses of radiation hundreds of times higher than would kill a person. It is easy to come away thinking they are indestructible, but they are not. Most of these feats apply only to dried tuns, and often only for short periods. An active, hydrated tardigrade is a fairly delicate animal, and studies suggest that long exposure to warm temperatures can kill one. Their real superpower is not toughness but the ability to switch life off and on."],
    ["Water bears in space", "In 2007, dried tardigrades became the first animals known to survive direct exposure to open space.",
      "In 2007 a European experiment launched dried tardigrades into low Earth orbit aboard an uncrewed Russian satellite. For about ten days they sat outside the spacecraft, exposed to the vacuum of space and cosmic radiation. Back on Earth, many were rehydrated and recovered, and some went on to reproduce. The ones that also faced unfiltered ultraviolet light from the Sun fared far worse, though a few still survived. It was the first time any animal was known to have survived open space. Tardigrades made headlines again in 2019, when an Israeli lunar lander carrying dried tardigrades crashed on the Moon. Later laboratory tests, which fired tardigrades from a gun at high speed, suggested the impact was probably too violent for them to have survived, so the Moon is likely not now home to water bears."],
    ["The secret ingredients", "Special proteins appear to turn tardigrade cells into a protective glass and shield their DNA from damage.",
      "How does an animal survive losing nearly all its water? Drying out normally destroys cells, because proteins unfold and membranes tear. Some other drought survivors, such as brine shrimp, flood their cells with a sugar called trehalose that forms a protective glassy coating. Many tardigrades make little of this sugar. Instead, researchers have found proteins unique to tardigrades that seem to form a gel and then a glass-like solid as the cell dries, holding delicate molecules in place until water returns. Another protein, named Dsup, short for 'damage suppressor', binds to DNA and appears to shield it. When scientists added the gene for Dsup to human cells growing in a lab, those cells suffered noticeably less DNA damage from X-rays than ordinary cells did."],
    ["Why scientists are interested", "Tardigrade tricks could one day help store medicines without refrigeration or protect cells from radiation.",
      "Tardigrades are not just a curiosity. Many vaccines and medicines must be kept cold from the factory to the clinic, which is expensive and difficult in hot, remote places, including parts of outback Australia. If the proteins tardigrades use to protect themselves could be harnessed, some medicines might be dried and stored at room temperature, then simply rehydrated when needed. Early laboratory studies have tested this idea with promising results. Researchers are also exploring whether tardigrade proteins could help protect crops from drought or shield cells from radiation, which matters for cancer treatment and long space missions. It is important to keep this in perspective: most of this work is at an early research stage, and turning a water bear's survival kit into practical technology may take many years."],
    ["Find one yourself", "With some moss, a dish of water and a cheap microscope, you can find tardigrades at home.",
      "One of the most delightful things about tardigrades is that you do not need a research ship or a laboratory to meet one. Collect a small clump of dry moss or lichen from a wall, roof, footpath crack or tree trunk. Place it in a shallow dish, cover it with rainwater or bottled water and leave it overnight. The next day, squeeze the moss over the dish and look at the water and sediment under a simple microscope, at about 20 to 40 times magnification. Be patient and scan slowly. You are looking for a small, chubby, slightly see-through animal clambering clumsily, rather like a bear in slow motion. It is a reminder that some of the toughest animals on the planet may be living in your gutter."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What does the scientific name Tardigrada mean?", ["Water bears", "Tiny survivors", "Moss dwellers", "Slow steppers"], 3, "Tardigrada means 'slow steppers', while 'water bear' is the popular nickname."],
    [1, "What is the dried, barrel-shaped state a tardigrade enters during drought called?", ["A cyst", "A pupa", "A tun", "A spore"], 2, "Tardigrades curl into a tun and enter cryptobiosis, with their metabolism almost stopped."],
    [2, "Which statement about tardigrade toughness is most accurate?", ["They are effectively indestructible at all times", "Active tardigrades survive boiling water indefinitely", "Their extreme tolerance applies mainly to the dried state", "They only survive cold, not heat or radiation"], 2, "Most of their famous feats apply to dried tuns, often only for short periods; active tardigrades are fairly delicate."],
    [4, "What does the tardigrade protein Dsup appear to do?", ["Digest prey", "Shield DNA from damage", "Make the animal glow", "Speed up reproduction"], 1, "Dsup binds to DNA and reduced X-ray damage when added to human cells in the lab."],
    [6, "What is a good way to look for tardigrades at home?", ["Dig deep into dry sand", "Soak moss in water and check it under a microscope", "Freeze a leaf and thaw it", "Look on the surface of a pond with a magnifying glass at night"], 1, "Soaking moss or lichen overnight and checking the water under a simple microscope often turns up tardigrades."]
  ]);

WB.L("mangroves", "nature", "beginner", "Mangroves: forests at the edge of the sea",
  "Muddy, smelly and buzzing with life, mangroves are trees that drink salt water, breathe through snorkels and quietly protect Australia's coasts.",
  [
    ["Trees that live in the sea", "Mangroves are salt-tolerant trees and shrubs that grow in the tidal zone of tropical and warm-temperate coasts.",
      "Walk along a boardwalk through a mangrove forest at low tide and you will hear the crackle of snapping shrimp, the pop of air bubbles in the mud and the scuttle of crabs ducking into holes. Twice a day the sea floods in and covers the roots. Mangroves are not one kind of plant but a mix of trees and shrubs from different families that have each adapted to life where land meets salt water. They grow along sheltered tropical and warm coastlines around the world. Australia has around 40 mangrove species and one of the largest areas of mangrove forest of any country, stretching across the north and down both the east and west coasts. Around Darwin, Cairns and the Gulf of Carpentaria, they form vast, dense forests."],
    ["Coping with salt", "Mangroves keep salt out at their roots, push it out through their leaves or lock it away in old leaves.",
      "Salt water kills most plants, because it draws water out of their cells. Mangroves have evolved several ways to cope. Many species filter salt out at the roots, so that the sap rising through the tree is almost fresh. Others take in salt and then get rid of it. The grey mangrove, the most widespread species in Australia, has special glands that push salt out through its leaves. Lick the underside of a grey mangrove leaf on a dry day and you may taste the salt crystals sitting on the surface. Some mangroves store excess salt in older leaves or bark, then shed them. These tricks are not free. They cost the plant energy, which is one reason mangroves often grow more slowly in very salty places."],
    ["Snorkels and stilts", "Special roots let mangroves breathe in airless mud and stand firm against the tide.",
      "Mangrove mud is soft, waterlogged and contains very little oxygen, yet roots need oxygen to survive. Mangroves have solved this with some of the strangest roots in the plant world. The grey mangrove sends up hundreds of pencil-like roots called pneumatophores that poke out of the mud like snorkels. Tiny pores on their surface take in air at low tide and pass it down to the buried roots. Red mangroves take a different approach, with arching prop roots that hold the trunk up above the water like a spider standing on its legs. Other species have knee-shaped or buttress roots. As well as helping the trees breathe, this tangle of roots anchors them in shifting mud and slows down the water flowing through, so that sediment settles out and builds up the shoreline."],
    ["Babies born ready", "Many mangroves germinate while still attached to the parent tree, then drop or float away as seedlings.",
      "Most plants drop their seeds and wait for them to sprout. Many mangroves do something closer to giving birth. The seed germinates while still on the parent tree, growing into a seedling called a propagule. In some species these look like long green cigars or pens dangling from the branches. When ready, the propagule drops. If the tide is out, it may lodge in the mud and start growing straight away. If the tide is in, it floats off and can drift for weeks or even months before washing up somewhere suitable. This head start helps the young plant establish quickly in a place where waves and tides would sweep away a tiny, ordinary seed. It is also how mangroves have spread along coastlines and between islands across the tropics."],
    ["Nurseries of the coast", "Mangroves shelter young fish, prawns and crabs, including many species Australians love to catch and eat.",
      "The calm water and tangled roots of a mangrove forest make an ideal nursery. Young fish and prawns can hide among the roots from larger predators while feeding on the rich supply of food produced as mangrove leaves fall and break down. Many species that support Australia's commercial and recreational fishing depend on mangroves for at least part of their lives, including barramundi, mud crabs, mangrove jack and banana prawns. Birds use mangroves too, from kingfishers and herons to migratory shorebirds feeding on the mudflats. The smell of rotten egg that you sometimes notice is not a sign of something wrong. It comes from bacteria breaking down organic matter in the oxygen-poor mud, part of the recycling that makes the whole system so productive."],
    ["Coastal guardians and carbon stores", "Mangroves soften waves, trap sediment and lock away large amounts of carbon in their muddy soils.",
      "Mangroves are a natural sea wall. Their dense trunks and roots absorb some of the energy of waves and storm surges, helping to reduce erosion and protect the land behind them. They also store carbon remarkably well. As leaves, roots and branches die, much of the material is buried in waterlogged mud where it breaks down very slowly. Over centuries this can build up into deep layers of carbon-rich soil. Mangroves, seagrass meadows and saltmarshes are often called 'blue carbon' ecosystems, and per hectare they can store several times more carbon than many land forests, most of it underground. When mangroves are cleared or drained, some of that stored carbon can be released into the atmosphere, which is one reason scientists and governments increasingly want to protect them."],
    ["Mangroves under pressure", "Clearing, coastal development and climate extremes threaten mangroves, but they also have legal protection.",
      "Around the world, large areas of mangroves have been cleared for shrimp farms, ports, housing and farmland. Australia's mangroves are in better shape than many, but they face pressures too. In late 2015 and early 2016, mangroves along roughly 1,000 kilometres of the Gulf of Carpentaria died back over a few months, affecting thousands of hectares. Scientists have linked the event to a combination of drought, high temperatures and a temporary drop in sea level during a strong El Niño. Some areas have since begun to recover. Mangroves are protected by law in Australian states and territories. In Queensland, for example, they are protected marine plants and it is an offence to damage them without approval, so check local rules before trimming any. The simplest way to appreciate them is from a boardwalk."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Roughly how many mangrove species does Australia have?", ["About 5", "About 400", "About 4,000", "About 40"], 3, "Australia has around 40 mangrove species and one of the world's largest areas of mangrove forest."],
    [1, "How does the grey mangrove get rid of excess salt?", ["Through glands in its leaves", "By storing it in its flowers", "By breathing it out at night", "It cannot tolerate salt at all"], 0, "Grey mangroves push salt out through leaf glands, leaving crystals on the surface."],
    [2, "What are pneumatophores?", ["Floating seeds", "Salt-storing leaves", "Snorkel-like roots that take in air", "Underground stems that store water"], 2, "Pneumatophores poke out of the mud and take in air for roots buried in oxygen-poor mud."],
    [4, "What usually causes the rotten egg smell in mangroves?", ["Dead fish", "Pollution from boats", "Bacteria breaking down matter in oxygen-poor mud", "Toxic mangrove flowers"], 2, "The smell comes from bacteria decomposing organic matter in low-oxygen mud, a natural process."],
    [6, "What was linked to the 2015 to 2016 mangrove dieback in the Gulf of Carpentaria?", ["A cyclone and an oil spill", "Drought, heat and a temporary drop in sea level", "Introduced goats", "A fungal disease spread by birds"], 1, "Scientists linked it to drought, high temperatures and lower sea levels during a strong El Niño."]
  ]);

WB.L("carnivorous-plants", "nature", "beginner", "Plants that eat animals",
  "Snapping traps, sticky tentacles and slippery pitchers: some plants have turned the tables on the animal world, and Australia is a hotspot for them.",
  [
    ["Why a plant would eat meat", "Carnivorous plants catch animals mainly for nutrients that are missing from the poor soils where they grow.",
      "Picture a peat bog: soggy, acidic and sunlit, with soil that holds almost no nitrogen or phosphorus. Most plants struggle there. Carnivorous plants thrive, because they have found another source of nutrients: animals. By trapping and digesting insects and other small creatures, they absorb the nitrogen, phosphorus and other minerals that the soil lacks. They still make their own food from sunlight, like any green plant. Prey is more like a fertiliser supplement than a main meal. There are several hundred carnivorous plant species, and the habit has evolved independently a number of times in unrelated plant groups, a sign of how useful it is in poor soils. Each group has come up with its own kind of trap, from snapping jaws to sticky glue to water-filled pits."],
    ["The Venus flytrap counts", "A Venus flytrap only snaps shut after its trigger hairs are touched more than once in quick succession.",
      "The Venus flytrap is the most famous carnivorous plant, yet in the wild it grows only in a small area of North and South Carolina in the United States. Each trap is a pair of hinged lobes lined with a few tiny trigger hairs. Touch one hair once and nothing happens. Touch a hair twice within about 20 seconds and the trap snaps shut in a fraction of a second. This simple counting system stops the plant wasting energy on raindrops or falling debris. Once the trap closes on an insect, the struggling prey keeps brushing the hairs, and further touches signal the plant to seal the trap tightly and release digestive juices. Digestion takes several days. Each trap can only open and close a limited number of times before it dies."],
    ["Pitchers: deadly pitfalls", "Pitcher plants lure insects into slippery, fluid-filled cups from which they cannot escape.",
      "Pitcher plants use a passive but very effective trap: a deep cup of leaf tissue holding a pool of digestive fluid. Nectar and colour lure insects to the rim, which in many species becomes extremely slippery when wet. Visitors lose their footing, tumble in and cannot climb the waxy inner walls. The tropical pitcher plants of the genus Nepenthes, found mostly in Southeast Asia, include giants whose pitchers can hold a litre or more of fluid. Some large species in Borneo have an unusual relationship with tree shrews, which lick sweet secretions from the pitcher lid and leave their droppings behind, giving the plant a useful dose of nitrogen. Australia has native Nepenthes too, growing in the far north of Queensland on Cape York Peninsula."],
    ["Sticky sundews", "Sundews trap insects with glistening droplets of glue, and Australia has a large share of the world's species.",
      "Sundews look as if they are covered in morning dew. Each leaf bristles with tentacles tipped with a glistening droplet, but the droplets are a sticky glue. An insect that lands is held fast, and in many species the tentacles slowly bend inward to press it against the leaf, where enzymes digest it. Sundews belong to the genus Drosera, and Australia is one of the world's great centres of sundew diversity, especially the south-west of Western Australia. Some Australian species are tiny 'pygmy sundews' no bigger than a coin, while others climb through shrubs on long stems. Many survive dry summers as underground tubers, sprouting again with winter rain. If you walk through heathland in Western Australia in winter or spring, look closely at the ground and you may spot them."],
    ["Underwater traps and an Australian original", "Bladderworts suck in prey in a split second, and the Albany pitcher plant grows nowhere else on Earth.",
      "Some of the most impressive carnivorous plants are almost invisible. Bladderworts live in water or wet soil and carry tiny bladders that pump out water to create low pressure inside. When a small creature such as a water flea brushes a trigger hair, a trapdoor swings open and the bladder sucks it in, all in a tiny fraction of a second. It is one of the fastest movements known in the plant kingdom. Australia also has a true original: the Albany pitcher plant, which grows wild only in a small coastal region of south-west Western Australia. It is so distinctive that it is the only species in its plant family. Its small, hairy pitchers sit at ground level, catching mostly ants and other crawling insects."],
    ["Threatened traps", "Habitat loss and illegal collecting threaten many carnivorous plants in the wild.",
      "Because carnivorous plants are specialists of nutrient-poor wetlands and heaths, they are vulnerable when those places change. Draining swamps, clearing land, adding fertiliser and suppressing the natural fires that keep competing plants in check can all push them out. Their striking looks also attract collectors. In the wild, the Venus flytrap's small range has made poaching a serious problem, and in North Carolina, taking the plants from the wild without permission has been treated as a felony since 2014. Several Nepenthes species in Southeast Asia are listed as threatened, partly because of illegal collecting. If you want to grow carnivorous plants, buy them from reputable nurseries that propagate their own stock, rather than plants that may have been taken from the wild. Never dig them up from bushland yourself."],
    ["Growing them at home", "Carnivorous plants need rainwater, poor soil and plenty of light, and they do not need feeding with meat.",
      "Carnivorous plants can make fascinating pets, but they break most of the usual gardening rules. Because they evolved in nutrient-poor soils, ordinary potting mix and fertiliser can burn their roots. Most are grown in a mix of peat moss or a peat substitute with sand or perlite. Tap water often contains enough dissolved minerals to harm them over time, so use rainwater or distilled water, and many types like to sit in a shallow tray of it. Most need plenty of bright light. Resist the urge to feed them pieces of meat, which can rot in the trap, and avoid setting off flytrap traps for fun, since every closure uses energy. Outdoors, the plants will usually catch all the insects they need on their own."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why do carnivorous plants catch animals?", ["Because they cannot photosynthesise", "To get water during droughts", "To protect their flowers from pests", "To get nutrients missing from poor soils"], 3, "They still make food from sunlight; prey mainly supplies nutrients like nitrogen and phosphorus."],
    [1, "What makes a Venus flytrap snap shut?", ["Any single touch on the leaf", "Sunlight hitting the trap", "Its trigger hairs being touched twice in quick succession", "Warm air from an animal's breath"], 2, "Two touches within about 20 seconds trigger the trap, which helps it ignore raindrops and debris."],
    [3, "Which part of Australia is a major centre of sundew diversity?", ["Tasmania's west coast", "South-west Western Australia", "The Nullarbor Plain", "Central Victoria"], 1, "South-west Western Australia is one of the world's great centres of sundew diversity."],
    [4, "What is special about the Albany pitcher plant?", ["It grows wild only in south-west Western Australia", "It is the largest pitcher plant in the world", "It catches small birds", "It lives entirely underwater"], 0, "It grows wild only in a small part of south-west WA and is the only species in its family."],
    [6, "What water is best for most carnivorous plants?", ["Tap water with fertiliser", "Salt water", "Rainwater or distilled water", "Sparkling mineral water"], 2, "They are sensitive to dissolved minerals, so rainwater or distilled water is best."]
  ]);

WB.L("camouflage", "nature", "beginner", "Masters of disguise: how animals hide",
  "A bird that becomes a broken branch, a fish that looks like seaweed, a cuttlefish that changes its skin in a blink: inside nature's art of hiding.",
  [
    ["The bird that is a branch", "Camouflage is about not being noticed, and it can rely on behaviour as much as colour.",
      "At dusk in an Australian park, you might walk right past a tawny frogmouth without seeing it. By day this bird sits motionless on a branch, its mottled grey and brown feathers matching the bark. If it senses danger, it stretches its body, points its beak upward, narrows its eyes to slits and freezes, so that it looks just like a broken stump. The disguise works because of the whole package: colour, shape and stillness. That is the heart of camouflage. It is not just about being the right colour. It is about defeating the eyes and brain of whoever is looking, whether that is a hungry predator or prey that needs to be ambushed. Over millions of years, animals have evolved many different ways to do it."],
    ["Blending in and countershading", "Many animals match their background and use countershading to flatten out the shadows that reveal their shape.",
      "The simplest strategy is background matching: being the same colour and pattern as your surroundings. A grasshopper on dry grass or a sand flathead on the seabed both use it. But there is a problem. Light usually comes from above, so the top of a body is brightly lit and the belly is in shadow, and that shading makes an object look solid and easy to pick out. Many animals counter this by being darker on top and paler underneath. This is called countershading, and it cancels out the shadow so the body looks flatter and fades into the background. Many fish, sharks and dolphins use it. Seen from above, their dark backs blend with the deep water below. Seen from beneath, their pale bellies blend with the bright surface."],
    ["Breaking up the outline", "Bold, contrasting patches can break up an animal's outline so that the eye does not recognise its shape.",
      "Our brains are very good at spotting outlines, so another strategy is to destroy them. This is disruptive colouration: bold patches of contrasting colour that cut across the edges of the body. The eye picks up the patches as separate shapes rather than one animal. Many moths, frogs and snakes use it, and stripes running across an eye can help hide one of the most recognisable features of all. Humans borrowed the idea. During the First World War, many ships were painted in striking stripes and zigzags known as dazzle camouflage. It was not meant to make them invisible at sea, which is almost impossible. Instead, the idea was to confuse enemy submarine crews trying to judge a ship's shape, speed and direction, so that their torpedoes would miss."],
    ["Pretending to be something else", "Some animals do not hide at all but look like an object a predator would ignore, such as a stick, leaf or seaweed.",
      "Some animals are perfectly visible but go unnoticed because they look like something boring or inedible. This is called masquerade. Stick insects look like twigs, and many sway gently as if moved by the wind. Leaf insects look like leaves, complete with fake veins and even nibbled edges. Some caterpillars and spiders look like bird droppings, which few predators want to eat. One of the most beautiful examples lives in southern Australia's cool waters. The leafy seadragon, a relative of seahorses, is covered in leaf-like flaps of skin and drifts slowly through kelp and seagrass, looking just like a piece of floating seaweed. It is the marine emblem of South Australia, and divers can spend a long time staring at weed before realising it is looking back at them."],
    ["Mimicry: borrowing a reputation", "Harmless animals can gain protection by copying the warning colours of dangerous ones.",
      "Not all disguises are about hiding. Many dangerous animals advertise themselves with bold warning colours, such as the yellow and black of wasps. Predators learn to avoid them. That opens a door for harmless species to cheat. Many hoverflies, which cannot sting, are striped yellow and black and look a lot like wasps or bees. This is called Batesian mimicry, after the naturalist Henry Walter Bates, who studied it among butterflies in the Amazon in the 1800s. Sometimes several genuinely dangerous species share the same warning pattern, which helps predators learn the lesson faster; this is called Mullerian mimicry. One of the most extraordinary mimics is the mimic octopus of Southeast Asia, which can change its shape and movement to resemble a flatfish, a venomous lionfish or a sea snake."],
    ["Cuttlefish: living screens", "Cuttlefish change colour in moments using thousands of tiny pigment sacs controlled by their nervous system.",
      "Cuttlefish, octopuses and squid have some of the most advanced camouflage on Earth. Their skin contains thousands of tiny elastic sacs of pigment called chromatophores, each surrounded by muscles controlled directly by nerves. By stretching or squeezing these sacs, a cuttlefish can change its colour and pattern in well under a second, and it can even raise bumps on its skin to match rough rock or weed. Strangely, most of these animals appear to be colour-blind, and scientists are still working out how they match colours so well. Each winter, around Point Lowly near Whyalla in South Australia, huge numbers of giant cuttlefish gather to breed. Some small males have been seen disguising themselves as females to slip past larger rival males and reach a mate, a trick right out of a spy story."],
    ["An endless arms race", "Camouflage evolves through natural selection, as predators and prey keep driving each other to improve.",
      "Camouflage is a product of natural selection. Individuals that are slightly harder to spot are more likely to survive and pass on their genes, while predators with sharper eyes catch more food. The result is an ongoing arms race. A classic example is the peppered moth in England. During the Industrial Revolution, soot darkened tree trunks, and a dark form of the moth, which was once rare, became common in industrial areas because birds found the pale moths easier to spot. After clean air laws reduced pollution in the twentieth century, the pale form became common again. Today camouflage faces new challenges, as climate change shifts the timing of seasons. In some places, animals that turn white for winter are now sometimes stranded in white coats on brown, snowless ground."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How does the tawny frogmouth hide when it senses danger?", ["It burrows underground", "It changes the colour of its feathers", "It freezes in a pose that resembles a broken branch", "It flies into dense grass"], 2, "It stretches upward, narrows its eyes and freezes, looking just like a broken stump."],
    [1, "What is countershading?", ["Being darker on top and paler underneath", "Changing colour with the seasons", "Copying a dangerous animal", "Having stripes that cross the eyes"], 0, "Dark backs and pale bellies cancel out the shading that would make a body look solid."],
    [2, "What was dazzle camouflage on First World War ships meant to do?", ["Make ships invisible", "Confuse judgements of a ship's speed and direction", "Reflect sunlight to blind pilots", "Identify friendly ships at night"], 1, "Dazzle patterns aimed to confuse submarine crews trying to judge a ship's course and speed."],
    [3, "Which animal masquerades as floating seaweed in southern Australian waters?", ["Blue-ringed octopus", "Leafy seadragon", "Moray eel", "Stingray"], 1, "The leafy seadragon's leaf-like flaps make it look like drifting seaweed."],
    [5, "What lets cuttlefish change colour so quickly?", ["Hormones released into the blood", "Bacteria living in their skin", "Absorbing pigments from their food", "Pigment sacs controlled by nerves and muscles"], 3, "Chromatophores are stretched or squeezed by muscles under direct nervous control."]
  ]);

WB.L("fungi", "nature", "intermediate", "The hidden kingdom of fungi",
  "The mushroom you see is just the tip of the iceberg. Below ground, fungi feed forests, recycle the dead and sometimes grow into some of the largest living things on Earth.",
  [
    ["Not quite plants", "Fungi are a kingdom of their own, more closely related to animals than to plants.",
      "After autumn rain, mushrooms seem to appear overnight on lawns and in bushland. It is tempting to think of them as odd little plants, but fungi are something else entirely. They cannot make food from sunlight. Instead, they feed by releasing digestive enzymes into their surroundings and absorbing the nutrients that are broken down. Their cell walls are made of chitin, the same tough material found in insect shells, not the cellulose of plants. Genetic studies show that fungi are actually more closely related to animals than to plants. The kingdom is vast and varied, including yeasts, moulds, mushrooms, rusts and lichen partners. Scientists have named around 150,000 species, but estimates of the true total run into the millions, and in Australia most fungi are thought to be still undescribed."],
    ["The real fungus is underground", "A mushroom is only a fruiting body; the main fungus is a network of fine threads called mycelium.",
      "A mushroom is to a fungus roughly what an apple is to an apple tree: a temporary structure for spreading spores. The main body of most fungi is the mycelium, a web of microscopic threads called hyphae that spread through soil, wood or leaf litter. A teaspoon of healthy soil can contain a startling length of these threads. Mycelium can also grow enormous. In a forest in Oregon in the United States, a single individual of a honey fungus has been found spreading through the soil across nearly ten square kilometres, and it is thought to be thousands of years old. It is often described as one of the largest living organisms on Earth. Above ground, all you would notice is the occasional clump of honey-coloured mushrooms and some unhealthy trees."],
    ["Partners of plants", "Most land plants rely on mycorrhizal fungi, which trade water and nutrients for sugars.",
      "Dig up almost any healthy plant and look closely at its roots, and you will probably find fungi wrapped around them or growing inside them. These partnerships are called mycorrhizas, from Greek words meaning 'fungus root', and the large majority of land plants form them. The deal is a trade. The fungus's fine threads reach far further through the soil than roots can, gathering water and nutrients such as phosphorus and nitrogen and passing them to the plant. In return, the plant feeds the fungus sugars made by photosynthesis. Many scientists think this partnership helped the first plants colonise dry land hundreds of millions of years ago. In Australia, many native plants have important fungal partners, and some orchids cannot even germinate from seed without the right fungus."],
    ["The 'wood wide web': what we know", "Fungal networks do connect plants, but popular claims about trees sharing and communicating go beyond the evidence.",
      "In 1997 the Canadian ecologist Suzanne Simard and colleagues published research suggesting that carbon could move between trees through shared fungal networks. A journal cover nicknamed it the 'wood wide web', and the idea took off. Books and documentaries now describe 'mother trees' feeding their seedlings and forests that warn each other of danger through underground networks. It is a beautiful story, and fungal networks linking the roots of different plants are real. However, how much material they move between trees, and whether this significantly helps them, is still debated. A 2023 review by a group of forest scientists argued that the evidence for some popular claims was weaker than often suggested. The careful view is that underground networks matter, but we are still learning how they really work."],
    ["Nature's recyclers", "Fungi break down dead wood and leaves, releasing nutrients that keep ecosystems running.",
      "Without fungi, forests would be buried in their own dead wood. Wood is tough to digest because it contains lignin, a complex material that stiffens plant cell walls. Few organisms can break lignin down efficiently, and some fungi, particularly the so-called white rot fungi, are among the best at it. As fungi, bacteria and small animals break down fallen logs, leaves and dead animals, they release nitrogen, phosphorus and other nutrients back into the soil for living plants to use. A rotting log in Australian bushland may look like waste, but it is a busy recycling plant and a home for beetles, frogs, lizards and small mammals. That is one reason many land managers now leave fallen timber on the ground rather than tidying it away."],
    ["Fungi in our lives", "Fungi give us bread, cheese, soy sauce and life-saving medicines like penicillin.",
      "You probably ate something made with fungi today. Baker's yeast is a single-celled fungus that releases carbon dioxide to make bread rise, and related yeasts ferment sugars into the alcohol in beer and wine. The blue veins in some cheeses are moulds from the genus Penicillium, and a mould called koji is used to make soy sauce, miso and sake. Mushrooms themselves are a valuable food. Fungi have also changed medicine. In 1928 Alexander Fleming noticed that a Penicillium mould was killing the bacteria on one of his laboratory dishes, which eventually led to penicillin, one of the first antibiotics. A drug that helps prevent organ rejection after transplants, cyclosporine, also came from a fungus. Researchers continue to search fungi for new medicines and enzymes."],
    ["The darker side, and staying safe", "Some fungi are deadly to eat or devastating to wildlife, so never eat wild mushrooms you cannot identify with certainty.",
      "Fungi have a darker side. Certain fungi infect insects and alter their behaviour; in some tropical forests, infected ants climb up a plant and clamp on before the fungus kills them and sprouts from their bodies. The chytrid fungus has caused declines in hundreds of frog species worldwide, including in Australia, and is linked to several frog extinctions here. Some fungi are deadly to people. The death cap mushroom, an introduced species found around Canberra and Melbourne, has caused deaths in Australia, and its toxins can destroy the liver. Many poisonous mushrooms look similar to edible ones, and cooking does not make them safe. This is general information, not advice: never eat wild mushrooms unless an expert has identified them, and if you suspect poisoning, call the Poisons Information Centre on 13 11 26."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Fungi are more closely related to which group?", ["Plants", "Animals", "Bacteria", "Algae"], 1, "Genetic studies show fungi are more closely related to animals than to plants."],
    [1, "What is mycelium?", ["The spore-producing cap of a mushroom", "A network of fine threads that forms the main body of a fungus", "A type of fungal toxin", "A chemical that helps plants photosynthesise"], 1, "Mycelium is the web of hyphae; the mushroom is just a temporary fruiting body."],
    [2, "What does a plant usually give its mycorrhizal fungus in return for water and nutrients?", ["Sugars made by photosynthesis", "Nitrogen from the air", "Protection from frost", "Seeds to spread"], 0, "The plant supplies sugars, while the fungus gathers water and nutrients like phosphorus."],
    [3, "What is the fairest summary of the 'wood wide web' idea?", ["It has been completely disproved", "Trees are proven to talk to each other like people", "Fungal networks are real, but some popular claims go beyond the evidence", "It only applies to tropical rainforests"], 2, "Networks linking roots exist, but how much they help trees is still debated."],
    [6, "If you suspect mushroom poisoning in Australia, who should you call?", ["The local council", "A gardening hotline", "Nobody, cooking makes mushrooms safe", "The Poisons Information Centre on 13 11 26"], 3, "Call the Poisons Information Centre on 13 11 26; cooking does not make poisonous mushrooms safe."]
  ]);

WB.L("ants", "nature", "intermediate", "Ants: the superorganism",
  "There may be around 20 quadrillion ants on Earth. Individually simple, together they farm, build, wage war and solve problems no single ant could.",
  [
    ["A planet of ants", "Scientists estimate around 20 quadrillion ants live on Earth, outweighing all wild birds and mammals combined.",
      "Sit on almost any patch of Australian ground for a few minutes and an ant will find you. That is not surprising when you look at the numbers. In 2022, researchers combined hundreds of studies from around the world and estimated that there are about 20 quadrillion ants on Earth, roughly two and a half million for every person. Together, they estimated, ants weigh more than all wild birds and wild mammals combined. More than 14,000 species have been named, and many more await description. Australia is especially rich in ants, with well over a thousand known species and probably many more. They live almost everywhere except the coldest places, from rainforest canopies to the hottest deserts, and they shape their environments by moving soil, spreading seeds and eating other insects."],
    ["The colony as one animal", "An ant colony works like a single organism, with a queen that reproduces and sterile workers doing everything else.",
      "To understand ants, it helps to stop thinking about individual ants. A typical colony is a family. At its heart is one or more queens, whose main job is laying eggs. Almost all the ants you see are workers, which are female and usually do not reproduce. Males appear only at certain times, mostly to fly off and mate, and they die soon after. Workers divide up the jobs: caring for young, building and defending the nest, and gathering food. In many species, an ant's job changes as it ages, with younger workers staying inside and older ones heading out to forage. Because the colony survives and reproduces as a unit, biologists often call it a superorganism, with workers acting a little like the cells of a body."],
    ["Smart without a leader", "Ants coordinate using chemical signals, and simple rules followed by many ants can produce clever group behaviour.",
      "No ant is in charge. The queen does not give orders, and no worker sees the big picture. Instead, ants communicate mainly with chemicals called pheromones. A forager that finds food lays a scent trail on the way home, and others follow it, adding their own scent if they also find food. Shorter routes get reinforced faster, because ants complete round trips more quickly, so the colony tends to settle on efficient paths without anyone planning them. This is an example of emergent behaviour, where intelligent-looking results arise from many individuals following simple rules. It has inspired computer scientists, who developed 'ant colony optimisation' methods for problems such as finding efficient routes. Ants also use touch, sound and alarm chemicals to warn nestmates of danger."],
    ["Farmers and herders", "Some ants grow fungus gardens and others tend insects for their sugary honeydew, long before humans farmed.",
      "Humans began farming roughly 12,000 years ago, but some ants have been doing it for tens of millions of years. The leafcutter ants of Central and South America do not eat the leaves they carry. Instead, they chew them into a pulp to feed a fungus that grows in underground gardens, and the fungus becomes their food. Their nests can contain millions of ants and cover large areas. Other ants are herders. They protect sap-sucking insects such as aphids and scale insects from predators and in return collect honeydew, a sugary liquid the insects excrete. Gardeners sometimes notice ants running up and down plants covered in scale insects, which is exactly this partnership in action. Some ants even carry their insect 'livestock' to fresh plants to feed."],
    ["Australian ant marvels", "Honeypot ants store nectar in their swollen bodies, and green tree ants sew leaves together with silk.",
      "Australia has some of the world's most remarkable ants. In the arid centre, honeypot ants have workers called repletes that are fed so much nectar and honeydew that their abdomens swell into round, amber-coloured balls the size of a small grape. They hang from the ceilings of underground chambers as living food stores for the colony in lean times. Aboriginal people have long dug them up as a sweet food. In the tropical north, green tree ants, also called weaver ants, build nests by pulling leaves together. Some workers hold the leaves in place while others carry larvae, which produce silk that is used to glue the leaves together. Green tree ants are also a traditional bush food, and many people describe their bodies as having a sharp, lemony taste."],
    ["Bites and stings", "Most ants are harmless to people, but bull ants and jack jumpers can cause serious allergic reactions.",
      "Most ants are a nuisance at worst, but Australia's bull ants and jack jumpers are worth respecting. These large, long-jawed ants have excellent eyesight, can be aggressive near their nests and deliver a painful sting. For most people the sting causes pain, swelling and redness that settles down. For a minority who are allergic, particularly to jack jumper venom, a sting can cause anaphylaxis, a severe whole-body reaction that can be life-threatening. Jack jumper allergy is especially well known in Tasmania. This is general information, not medical advice. Signs such as difficulty breathing, swelling of the face or throat, or collapse need urgent help, so call 000. People with a known allergy should follow their doctor's plan, and healthdirect has reliable information on insect stings and allergic reactions."],
    ["Invaders", "Invasive ants such as red imported fire ants and yellow crazy ants can devastate wildlife, farms and lifestyles.",
      "Some of the world's most damaging invasive species are ants. Red imported fire ants, originally from South America, were found in Brisbane in 2001 and have been the target of a major eradication effort ever since. They deliver painful stings, can harm people, pets, livestock and wildlife, and could cost Australia's economy and environment heavily if they spread widely. On Christmas Island, introduced yellow crazy ants formed huge 'supercolonies' and killed millions of the island's famous red crabs, prompting long-running control programs. Invasive ants often succeed because many colonies cooperate rather than fight, and because they arrive without their natural enemies. If you live in an affected area, learn what fire ants and their nests look like, and report suspected sightings to the national fire ant program or your state biosecurity agency."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "According to a 2022 estimate, how do ants compare with wild birds and mammals by weight?", ["Ants weigh far less", "Ants weigh about the same as wild birds alone", "Ants weigh more than wild birds and mammals combined", "Nobody has tried to estimate it"], 2, "The study estimated about 20 quadrillion ants, outweighing all wild birds and mammals combined."],
    [1, "Which ants make up almost all of the ants you see in a colony?", ["Queens", "Male drones", "Female workers", "Soldier males"], 2, "Workers are female and usually do not reproduce; males appear mainly to mate."],
    [2, "How do ant colonies tend to find efficient routes to food?", ["The queen plans the route", "Shorter trails get reinforced with pheromones faster", "Scouts draw maps in the soil", "They follow the sun's position only"], 1, "Ants on shorter routes return sooner, reinforcing those scent trails more quickly."],
    [4, "What do honeypot ant repletes do?", ["Guard the nest entrance", "Sew leaves with silk", "Farm fungus underground", "Store nectar and honeydew in swollen abdomens"], 3, "Repletes hang in underground chambers as living food stores for the colony."],
    [6, "What should you do if you suspect red imported fire ants?", ["Dig up the nest to check", "Ignore them, they are harmless", "Report it to the fire ant program or state biosecurity agency", "Move the nest to bushland"], 2, "Suspected sightings should be reported so the eradication program can respond."]
  ]);

WB.L("eucalypts", "nature", "intermediate", "Eucalypts: trees built for fire",
  "Gum trees define the Australian landscape. Their oils, bark and hidden buds tell the story of a continent shaped by fire.",
  [
    ["Australia's signature tree", "Eucalypts dominate most of Australia's forests and woodlands, with hundreds of species.",
      "Step out into the bush after rain and you may catch it: the sharp, clean scent of eucalyptus oil rising from the leaves. Gum trees are so familiar that it is easy to forget how remarkable they are. Botanists group the eucalypts into three closely related genera: Eucalyptus, Corymbia, which includes many bloodwoods and the spotted gums, and Angophora. Together they include more than 800 species, almost all native to Australia, with a few in New Guinea and nearby islands. They dominate roughly three-quarters of Australia's native forests, and they grow in almost every environment, from snowy mountains to the dry inland. Over millions of years, as the continent dried out and fire became more common, eucalypts became extraordinarily good at living with both drought and flame."],
    ["From mallees to giants", "Eucalypts range from shrubby mallees to mountain ash, the tallest flowering plants on Earth.",
      "Eucalypts come in a dazzling range of forms. In semi-arid southern Australia, mallees grow as clumps of slender stems from a single swollen base, often only a few metres tall. At the other extreme is the mountain ash of Victoria and Tasmania, the world's tallest flowering plant. A mountain ash in southern Tasmania known as Centurion has been measured at about 100 metres tall, taller than a 25-storey building. Their bark varies just as much. Stringybarks have thick, fibrous bark, ironbarks have hard, deeply furrowed black bark, and smooth-barked gums shed their outer layer each year to reveal fresh, colourful trunks underneath. The famous scribbly gums carry winding tracks on their bark, made by the larvae of tiny moths tunnelling between the old and new layers."],
    ["Oil in the leaves", "Eucalyptus leaves are full of aromatic oils that deter many animals and are highly flammable.",
      "Hold a eucalyptus leaf up to the light and you may see tiny translucent dots. These are oil glands. The oils are a mix of aromatic compounds that help protect the leaves from many insects and animals that would otherwise eat them. Only specialists, such as koalas, some possums and certain insects, can cope with large amounts. People have used eucalyptus oil for generations in cleaning products and remedies for colds. The oils are also flammable, and on hot days some of these compounds evaporate from the leaves. Along with other natural chemicals released by the trees, they are thought to contribute to the blue haze that gives the Blue Mountains near Sydney their name. That same volatility helps explain why eucalypt forests can burn so fiercely in extreme weather."],
    ["Hidden buds and swollen roots", "Many eucalypts survive fire by resprouting from buds protected under their bark or from woody lumps at their base.",
      "A few months after a bushfire, the blackened trunks of many eucalypt forests become covered in soft green and red shoots. This is one of the most striking sights in Australian nature. Many eucalypts have epicormic buds, dormant buds hidden beneath their thick bark along the trunk and branches. Fire may kill the leaves, but the insulated buds survive and sprout once the danger has passed, rebuilding the canopy far faster than a seedling could. Many species also have a lignotuber, a woody swelling at the base of the trunk, partly underground, packed with buds and food reserves. If the whole tree above ground is destroyed, it can resprout from this lump. Mallees are a classic example, regrowing from their lignotubers again and again after fire."],
    ["The trees that must burn and wait", "Some eucalypts, such as mountain ash, are killed by intense fire and rely on seed to regenerate.",
      "Not all eucalypts resprout. Mountain ash and its close relative alpine ash are usually killed by intense fire. Instead, they bet on their seeds. Their capsules, the gumnuts, hold seeds that are released in huge numbers after the heat of a fire. The seeds fall onto a bed of nutrient-rich ash in full sunlight, and a dense new forest springs up, with all the trees roughly the same age. This strategy works well when big fires come at long intervals. The danger comes when fires return too often. Young mountain ash take many years, often around two decades, to produce plenty of seed. If a young forest burns again before then, there may be no seed left, and the forest can be replaced by shrubs or other species."],
    ["People and fire", "Aboriginal people have shaped Australian landscapes with fire for tens of thousands of years.",
      "Fire in Australia is not only a natural force. Aboriginal people have used it as a land management tool for tens of thousands of years. Traditional burning, often called cultural burning, typically involves cooler, smaller fires lit at chosen times of year to create a patchwork of burnt and unburnt country. This can reduce fuel, encourage fresh growth that attracts animals and keep paths through the land open. Many of these practices were disrupted after colonisation. Today, a growing number of land managers work with Aboriginal rangers and communities to bring cultural burning back. The Black Summer fires of 2019 and 2020, which burned millions of hectares, highlighted how hotter, drier conditions linked to climate change are making fire seasons more dangerous, and how much we still need to learn."],
    ["Gum trees at home and abroad", "Eucalypts are planted worldwide, and some can drop large branches without warning.",
      "Eucalypts have spread far beyond Australia. Fast-growing species have been planted in many countries, including California, Portugal, Brazil, India and parts of Africa, for timber, paper pulp and fuel. In some places they are valued plantation trees; in others they are criticised for using a lot of water or for adding to fire risk where they have spread. At home, it is worth knowing that some gums, particularly river red gums and other large eucalypts, can shed big limbs suddenly, sometimes on calm, hot days with no warning. That has earned them the grim nickname 'widowmaker'. When camping, parking or picnicking, avoid setting up directly under large overhanging branches. For bushfire preparation, follow your state fire service's advice, which explains how to manage trees and vegetation around your home."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Roughly what share of Australia's native forests are dominated by eucalypts?", ["About one-tenth", "About one-quarter", "About three-quarters", "Almost none"], 2, "Eucalypts dominate roughly three-quarters of Australia's native forests."],
    [1, "What is special about mountain ash?", ["It is the world's tallest flowering plant", "It grows only in deserts", "It never sheds its bark", "It is the only eucalypt found outside Australia"], 0, "Mountain ash is the tallest flowering plant, with one Tasmanian tree measured at about 100 metres."],
    [3, "What are epicormic buds?", ["Seeds stored in gumnuts", "Flowers that open only after rain", "Oil glands in the leaves", "Dormant buds under the bark that sprout after fire"], 3, "Protected under thick bark, they sprout after fire and rebuild the canopy quickly."],
    [4, "Why can frequent fires threaten mountain ash forests?", ["Their bark becomes too thick", "Young trees may be burned before they produce enough seed", "Fire makes their seeds too heavy to spread", "They resprout too slowly from lignotubers"], 1, "Mountain ash rely on seed and take many years to produce plenty of it."],
    [6, "Why do some large gums carry the nickname 'widowmaker'?", ["Their oil is poisonous", "They can drop large branches suddenly", "Their roots damage houses", "They attract dangerous snakes"], 1, "Some large gums, like river red gums, can shed big limbs without warning."]
  ]);

WB.L("animal-longevity", "nature", "advanced", "Animals that cheat ageing",
  "A shark born before Captain Cook sailed, a rodent that barely seems to age, a jellyfish that turns back its own clock: what nature's longest lives reveal about growing old.",
  [
    ["Ming the clam", "Some animals live for centuries, and their ages can be read from growth layers in shells and bodies.",
      "In 2006, scientists dredging the seabed off Iceland pulled up an ordinary-looking clam. Like the rest of the haul, it was frozen on board as routine, so it was already dead when, back in the lab, scientists counted the growth rings in its shell, much as you would count the rings of a tree, and realised it was extraordinary. The clam, an ocean quahog later nicknamed Ming, turned out to be about 507 years old. It had been alive since around the start of the 1500s. Ming is one of the oldest individual animals whose age has been reliably measured. Its story raises a big question that has puzzled biologists for generations. If some animals can live for centuries, why do others, including us, age and die on a much shorter schedule? Nature offers some surprising clues."],
    ["Why do we age at all?", "Evolutionary theory suggests ageing happens because natural selection weakens later in life.",
      "From an evolutionary point of view, ageing is a puzzle. Why would natural selection allow bodies to wear out? Biologists such as Peter Medawar and George Williams developed an influential answer in the mid twentieth century. In the wild, most animals die young from predators, disease, accidents or starvation. Genes that cause problems late in life are rarely 'seen' by natural selection, because few individuals live long enough for them to matter. Some genes that help early in life, for example by boosting reproduction, might even cause harm later. A related idea, the 'disposable soma' theory, suggests bodies invest in reproduction rather than perfect long-term repair. The theory makes a prediction: animals that face less danger from the outside world should evolve to age more slowly. Many long-lived species fit that pattern."],
    ["Giants of the cold", "Greenland sharks and bowhead whales may live for centuries in cold Arctic waters.",
      "In the cold, dark waters of the North Atlantic and Arctic lives the Greenland shark, a predator that can grow to several metres long. Its age is hard to measure, because sharks lack the bony growth rings of many fish. In 2016, researchers used radiocarbon dating on the lenses of the sharks' eyes, which form before birth and do not change. They estimated that the largest shark in their study was around 400 years old, although with a wide margin of uncertainty. That would make it the longest-lived vertebrate known. The bowhead whale, another Arctic giant, may live for more than 200 years. Old harpoon tips from the 1800s have been found embedded in whales caught in modern times. Cold temperatures, slow growth and large bodies seem to go together with very long lives."],
    ["The naked mole-rat", "Naked mole-rats can live for over 30 years, rarely get cancer and do not seem to become more likely to die as they age.",
      "The naked mole-rat of East Africa is pink, wrinkled and almost hairless, and it may be one of the most intriguing animals in ageing research. A mouse of similar size usually lives two or three years. Naked mole-rats have lived for more than 30 years in captivity. Even more surprising, a study of thousands of animals found that their risk of dying did not seem to rise as they got older, unlike almost every other mammal, including humans, whose risk of death climbs steadily with age. They also rarely develop cancer. Naked mole-rats live in large underground colonies, protected from many predators, which fits the evolutionary theory of ageing. Scientists are studying their cells, proteins and metabolism closely to work out which of their traits might explain their resilience."],
    ["Turning back the clock", "Some simple animals, like the 'immortal jellyfish' and hydra, show little or no sign of ageing.",
      "Some of the most dramatic examples come from simple animals. A tiny jellyfish called Turritopsis dohrnii has been nicknamed the immortal jellyfish. When it is injured, stressed or old, it can transform back into an earlier life stage, a polyp attached to the seabed, and then grow into a new jellyfish again. In principle, it could repeat this cycle many times. In practice, individuals still die from predators and disease, so it is not truly immortal. The freshwater hydra, a small relative of jellyfish, also appears to show little or no ageing. Its body is constantly renewed by stem cells, and in laboratory studies hydra did not show the usual increase in death rate over time. These animals suggest that ageing is not an unbreakable law of biology."],
    ["The cancer puzzle", "Big, long-lived animals have far more cells but do not get more cancer, a mystery called Peto's paradox.",
      "Cancer starts when a cell picks up harmful mutations and begins dividing out of control. So you might expect animals with more cells, living longer, to get more cancer. An elephant has vastly more cells than a mouse, and a blue whale more still. Yet large animals do not seem to have higher cancer rates than small ones. This puzzle is known as Peto's paradox, after the English epidemiologist Richard Peto. Some answers are emerging. Elephants have around 20 copies of a gene called TP53, which helps detect damaged DNA and trigger the death of faulty cells, while humans have just one pair. Bowhead whales appear to be unusually good at repairing damaged DNA. Large animals seem to have evolved extra defences that let them grow big and live long."],
    ["What it means for us", "Long-lived animals inspire ageing research, but there is no proven shortcut to a much longer human life.",
      "Long-lived animals are giving researchers real leads, from DNA repair to how cells manage damaged proteins. Bats are another useful example: some small bats live for several decades, far longer than mammals their size usually do. Even a fish from Queensland made the record books. An Australian lungfish nicknamed Granddad lived at an aquarium in Chicago for more than 80 years and was thought to be over 90 when he died. It is important to be realistic, though. These discoveries do not yet translate into treatments that extend human life, and many products marketed as anti-ageing are not backed by good evidence. This is general information, not health advice. For now, the well-tested basics, such as not smoking, staying active, sleeping well and eating well, remain the best supported."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How was the age of Ming the clam worked out?", ["Counting growth rings in its shell", "Radiocarbon dating of its eye", "Measuring its weight", "Studying its DNA length"], 0, "Like tree rings, the growth layers in its shell revealed an age of about 507 years."],
    [1, "According to evolutionary theory, which animals should evolve to age more slowly?", ["Those facing more danger from predators", "Those facing less danger from the outside world", "Those that reproduce earliest", "Those with the smallest bodies"], 1, "When fewer individuals die young, selection favours better long-term maintenance."],
    [2, "How did scientists estimate the age of Greenland sharks?", ["Counting rings in their fins", "Tagging them for 400 years", "Measuring tooth wear", "Radiocarbon dating of their eye lenses"], 3, "Eye lenses form before birth and do not change, so radiocarbon dating them gave age estimates."],
    [3, "What is unusual about the naked mole-rat's risk of dying?", ["It drops sharply after age two", "It doubles every year", "It does not seem to rise as the animal ages", "It is higher than any other rodent"], 2, "A large study found their risk of death did not climb with age, unlike most mammals."],
    [5, "What is Peto's paradox?", ["Large animals do not get more cancer despite having more cells", "Small animals live longer than large ones", "Cold-water animals age faster", "Jellyfish can reverse their life cycle"], 0, "Elephants and whales have far more cells than mice but not higher cancer rates."]
  ]);
